<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Poland (1025-1385)</h1><p class="st-meta">1025 CE – 1385 CE · capital Krakow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Piast kingdom of Poland, which fragmented into rival duchies, was reunited in 1320 and flourished under Casimir the Great.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Boleslaw I the Brave</a> was crowned king in 1025, turning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-poland">Duchy of Poland</a> into a kingdom. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-lambert">Mieszko II</a> faced invasion and revolt, and in the 1030s a pagan uprising and a Bohemian raid devastated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Greater Poland</a>. Casimir I the Restorer rebuilt the state and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> its main seat.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Boleslaw III</a> in 1138 the realm was divided among his sons, with the senior duke ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a>. For almost two centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was a patchwork of duchies, settled by German colonists and towns under German law and hit by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1241. The church, with the archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, held it together.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high</a> reunited much of the country and was crowned in 1320. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> codified law, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">University of Krakow</a> in 1364 and added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>. After Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Jadwiga</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Union of Krewo</a> of 1385, beginning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1385-1569).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Poland (1025-1385)" width="320" height="500"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_coat_of_arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Poland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1025 CE – 1385 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Krakow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Boleslaw I the Brave, Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high, Casimir III the Great</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Piast Poland, Kingdom of Poland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1205&amp;focus=kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385" aria-label="Kingdom of Poland (1025-1385) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 436 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Poland (1025-1385) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5457 692L5446 783M5457 692L5387 662M5457 692L5324 597M5457 692L5571 720M5457 692L5395 622M5457 692L5330 692M5457 692L5457 692M5457 692L5457 692M5457 692L5380 625M5457 692L5496 674M5457 692L5442 621M5457 692L5409 614M5457 692L5424 620M5457 692L5432 635M5457 692L5424 648M5457 692L5376 568"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5446 783h0M5387 662h0M5324 597h0M55